Package: sphinx
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: all
Maintainer: Sentinel Safeguard LLC <Sentinelsafeguard@outlook.com>
Depends: python3 (>= 3.10), systemd, wireguard-tools
Recommends: openvpn
Section: net
Priority: optional
Homepage: https://github.com/TheRealJesusTheHacker/Sphinx
Description: Sphinx — system-wide ad and tracker blocker (Debian edition, Phase 2)
 Every request gets questioned. Sphinx filters the DNS lookups this
 machine makes: names on its blocklist are answered locally with a
 sinkhole (0.0.0.0), and everything else is forwarded to the upstream
 resolver unchanged. It sees names only — it never inspects, decrypts,
 or modifies the contents of any connection.
 .
 The filter daemon (a systemd service), the bundled blocklist, and
 the engage/restore commands that point this machine's DNS at the
 filter and put it back exactly. On top of that core: manual list
 updates from Sphinx's shown sources (HTTPS only, sanity-gated,
 atomic — nothing fetches in the background), your own allow/block
 rules, and named profiles of those rules.
 Installing this package never changes your DNS by itself: the
 service is not started or enabled by the install, and turning
 Sphinx on is a separate, explicit step (sudo sphinx engage).
 There is no graphical interface yet. Ads served from the same
 domain as the content itself (YouTube, for example) cannot be
 blocked by any DNS filter; Sphinx does not claim otherwise.
